// Copy to LocalSigning.xcconfig (untracked) to sign local macOS builds with a
// stable identity instead of ad-hoc.
//
// Why it matters: an ad-hoc signature has no identity beyond the hash of the
// binary, so every rebuild is a different application to macOS. The login
// keychain stores an ACL naming the exact hashes it trusts, and TCC keys the
// microphone grant the same way — so each build re-prompts, and a build the
// user has not approved is simply refused. The keychain ACL on the machine
// where this was diagnosed had thirteen entries, one per rebuild.
//
// Signing with a certificate makes the designated requirement name the
// certificate instead of the hash:
//
//   identifier "ai.augustyniak.capture" and certificate leaf = H"<cert hash>"
//
// which survives rebuilds, and is shared by every worktree building the same
// bundle identifier.
//
// A self-signed certificate is enough and costs nothing — Keychain Access →
// Certificate Assistant → Create a Certificate, Identity Type "Self Signed
// Root", Certificate Type "Code Signing". Confirm it is usable with:
//
//   security find-identity -v -p codesigning
//
// Then put its exact name below. Leave this file absent to keep ad-hoc
// signing; nothing else in the build depends on it.
LOCAL_SIGN_IDENTITY = Your Signing Identity Name
